  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 412 PM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nA modest 60-75 kt jet streak at 500-mb looks to be located north \n\\and east of the forecast region Wednesday morning, as a trough \nsweeps through the Great Lakes Region. A surface low in the right \nentrance region of this upper-level jet would be favored just to \nthe south of the area, providing northerly surface winds at the \nstart of the morning. These winds may gradually shift out of the \neast throughout the day as a surface high moves east across the \nNorthern Plains. Even so, forecast highs are currently in the mid \n80s to lower 90s, slightly cooler than recent days. Highs could be\nhotter if the surface low does not develop as far south as \ncurrent guidance shows, or if the northerly to easterly winds \nthroughout the day are on the lighter side, allowing solar heating\nto play a more prominent role in the high temperatures. This is \nrepresented in the NBM 75th percentile temperatures, which reach \ninto the mid and upper 90s across southern portions of the County \nWarning Area (CWA). There is a chance for showers and \nthunderstorms to develop Wednesday evening, and possibly lasting \ninto the overnight hours. Forecast guidance shows PoPs \n(probability of precipitation) in the 20s and 30s across most of \nthe area during this period. However, NBM 24 hr precipitation \nguidance shows a 1 in 3 chance or less of receiving greater than \n0.1 inches of rain for all areas in the CWA. The likelihood that \nany storm becomes severe Wednesday evening is low, as LREF \nguidance shows less than a 40% chance across the entire area that \nsfc-500mb shear will exceed 30 kts. The best opportunity for \nsevere storms would be across portions of Southwest Nebraska, \nwhere these probabilities are in the 30s. Even so, confidence that\nsevere weather occurs anywhere in the area Wednesday is 5% or \nless at this time.\n\nBy Thursday morning, troughing is favored to be off to the west, as \nridging begins to move in overhead. Surface winds look to be out \nof the south during this period, helping highs to stay in the \nlower 90s across the area. Chances for precipitation begin to \ndecrease as well, with PoPs below 20% throughout the entire day. \nIf storms are able to develop, they would most likely be \nassociated with convergence across eastern parts of the area, or \nfrom storms moving into portions of Eastern Colorado from the \nFront Range. LREF guidance shows slightly better probabilities for\nsfc-500mb wind shear greater than 30 kts as opposed to Wednesday,\nbut still under 50% across the forecast area. Once again, \nconfidence that severe weather occurs in the area Thursday is 5% \nor less.\n\nForecast guidance begins to diverge on Friday. Ensemble guidance \nseems to have the most confidence in a deep, longwave trough forming \nacross the Northeast. This could support an active weather pattern, \nwith multiple shortwave systems traversing the forecast area. \nDepending on the timing of these shortwave features, high \ntemperatures between the mid 80s and upper 90s could be expected. \nThe higher end of this range would be more likely if the forecast \nregion were along the leading edge of a shortwave trough during \nthe afternoon, providing sufficient southwesterly flow for warmer \nconditions. However, if a shortwave trough is directly overhead \nduring the afternoon, conditions may be cooler. Precipitation \ncontinues to look unlikely during this period as well.",
